<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Damkina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Damgalnuna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Damkina, also called Damgalnuna, was a Mesopotamian mother goddess, the wife of the god Ea (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a>), lord of fresh water and wisdom. In the Babylonian creation epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enuma-elish">Enuma Elish</a> she is the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>. She was worshipped with Ea across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, including at Malgium on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damkina.jpg" alt="Damkina" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yazilikaya52-55.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name Damgalnuna can be translated as the great wife of the prince, the prince being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a>. Its writing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> sign NUN, used for the name of Eridu, reflects her tie to that city. The form Damkina developed later, late sources spell it Damkianna, and the Hurrian form was Tapkina. Beyond her marriage her character was poorly defined. Believers held that she interceded with Enki for human supplicants, and like him she was linked with purification and exorcism and invoked against demons. In astronomy she was identified with the Wagon of Heaven, Ursa Minor, and lions may have been her animals.</p><p>Nanshe, Asalluhi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and Enbilulu were counted among the children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> and Damgalnuna, and the deities Ḫasīsu and Uznu, wisdom and ear, were her attendants. The myth Enki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a> treats her as the same goddess as Ninhursag, though the two were usually separate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Her worship is attested in every period of Mesopotamian history, though it gradually narrowed. The Early Dynastic Zame Hymns name Eridu as her cult centre. At Malgium, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> lists her among the city's deities, and its rulers Takil-ilišu and Ipiq-Ishtar called themselves appointed by her and Ea. Shulgi built her a temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>. On Old Babylonian seals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> she and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> are among the three divine couples most often invoked, and an inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> shows that the pair shared a temple at Kalhu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>She entered Hurrian religion as the wife of Ea, perhaps as early as the third millennium BCE according to Piotr Taracha. She is named among the divine witnesses of the treaty between Šattiwaza and Šuppiluliuma I and appears in the procession of goddesses at Yazılıkaya.</p><p>The only known myth centred on her, which Wilfred G. Lambert titled Damkina's Bond, tells of a conflict between the gods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, ended by her intervention after a messenger brought her word of the battle. Lambert dated it to the Kassite or Isin II dynasty and thought it came from Malgium; Takayoshi Oshima holds it is no earlier than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-i">Nebuchadnezzar I</a>. In the Enūma Eliš she appears as Marduk's mother under the name Damkina, without a genealogy of her own.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Damgalnuna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its kings, such as Ipiq-Ishtar and Takil-ilissu, left Akkadian building inscriptions recording temples for Ea and <b>Damkina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/malgium">Malgium</a></li></ul></section>
